# Lab 04: Test Firewall Rule Persistence

## Objective

As `user1` with `sudo` privileges on `server30`, test the difference between runtime and permanent firewalld configurations by first adding TCP port `9000` as a runtime-only rule and verifying that it disappears after a reboot. Then add the same rule permanently and verify that it persists after another reboot.

## Steps

Add TCP port `9000` to the runtime configuration of the default zone:

sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --add-port=9000/tcp

Verify that the port is currently allowed:

sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --list-ports

The output should include:

9000/tcp

Reboot the system:

sudo reboot

After the system restarts, verify that port `9000` is no longer present:

sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --list-ports

Because the rule was added without the `--permanent` option, it existed only in the runtime configuration and was lost when the system rebooted.

Now add the same rule permanently:

sudo firewall-cmd --permanent --zone=public --add-port=9000/tcp

Reload firewalld to activate the permanent rule:

sudo firewall-cmd --reload

Verify that the rule is active:

sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --list-ports

The output should include:

9000/tcp

Reboot the system again:

sudo reboot

After the system restarts, verify the port once more:

sudo firewall-cmd --zone=public --list-ports

The output should still include:

9000/tcp

## Result

The first `9000/tcp` rule was added only to the runtime configuration and disappeared after reboot. The second rule was added with `--permanent`, activated with `--reload`, and remained present after reboot. This demonstrated the difference between firewalld's runtime and permanent configurations.
